sections.s2_technical:
Functional requirements are the features. For a banking transfer system they include "a user can transfer money," "a user can view their balance," "a user can download a statement," and "a user receives a notification after a transfer." Each one is testable with a simple check: did the transfer happen, is the balance shown. You can model them as use cases or user stories, and they map directly to API endpoints.

```java
// Functional requirements map to endpoints and behaviour
public interface BankingApi {
    TransferResult transfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount);  // "user can transfer"
    BigDecimal balance(String accountId);                                // "user can view balance"
    Statement statement(String accountId, LocalDate from, LocalDate to); // "user can download statement"
}
```

Non-functional requirements are the quality attributes, and the four most discussed in interviews are latency, availability, consistency, and scalability. Latency is how fast a single request returns, usually expressed as a percentile like "p99 under two hundred milliseconds," meaning ninety-nine percent of requests are faster than that. Availability is the fraction of time the system is up, expressed in nines, where three nines is about forty-three minutes of downtime per month and five nines is about twenty-six seconds. Consistency is whether every reader sees the latest write; banking demands strong consistency while a social feed tolerates eventual consistency. Scalability is whether the system keeps its latency and availability as load grows.

```java
// Non-functional requirements expressed as measurable targets
public record ServiceLevelObjective(
    long p99LatencyMs,        // e.g. 200 ms
    double availability,      // e.g. 0.9999 (four nines)
    boolean strongConsistency,// true for money
    long targetPeakQps        // e.g. 6000
) {}
```

A senior nuance is that non-functional requirements usually conflict and force trade-offs. Strong consistency fights availability during a network partition, as the CAP theorem states. Low latency fights durability if you acknowledge writes before they are safely replicated. The job of a designer is to make these trade-offs explicitly and in line with the domain: for money you favour consistency and durability even at the cost of some latency, while for a trending-posts feed you favour availability and speed and accept slightly stale data. Writing non-functional targets as numbers is what lets you later prove the system met them.

