// ─── File: UrlShortenerService.java ───
package com.bank.shortener;

import java.time.LocalDateTime;
import java.util.logging.*;

public class UrlShortenerService {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger("Shortener");
    private final MappingRepository repo;
    private final Cache cache;
    private final Base62Codec codec;

    public UrlShortenerService(MappingRepository repo, Cache cache, Base62Codec codec) {
        this.repo = repo; this.cache = cache; this.codec = codec;
    }

    // create a short key; customAlias and expiry are optional (nullable)
    public String shorten(String longUrl, String customAlias, LocalDateTime expiry, String owner) {
        long id = repo.nextId();
        String key;
        if (customAlias != null) {
            if (repo.keyExists(customAlias))
                throw new IllegalStateException("Alias already taken: " + customAlias);
            key = customAlias;
        } else {
            key = codec.encode(id);
        }
        repo.save(new UrlMapping(id, key, longUrl, owner, expiry));
        log.info(() -> "Stored mapping " + key + " -> " + longUrl);
        return key;
    }

    // cache-aside resolve; records a click; honours expiry
    public String resolve(String shortKey) {
        String cached = cache.get(shortKey);
        if (cached != null) {
            log.fine(() -> "cache HIT " + shortKey);
            recordClick(shortKey);
            return cached;
        }
        log.fine(() -> "cache MISS " + shortKey);
        UrlMapping m = repo.findByKey(shortKey);
        if (m == null) { log.warning(() -> "Unknown key " + shortKey); return null; }
        if (m.isExpired()) { log.warning(() -> "Expired key " + shortKey); return null; }
        cache.put(shortKey, m.getLongUrl());
        recordClick(shortKey);
        return m.getLongUrl();
    }

    private void recordClick(String key) {
        UrlMapping m = repo.findByKey(key);
        if (m != null) m.incrementClicks(); // in production: async via Kafka
    }

    public long clickCount(String key) {
        UrlMapping m = repo.findByKey(key);
        return m == null ? 0 : m.getClicks();
    }
}
```

sections.s2_technical:
The fixed window algorithm divides time into fixed intervals, say one minute, and counts requests in the current interval. It is simple but has a boundary flaw: a client can send the full limit at the end of one minute and the full limit at the start of the next, effectively doubling the rate for a moment.

```java
// Fixed window: a counter per client per minute
public boolean allowFixed(String client, int limit) {
    long minute = System.currentTimeMillis() / 60_000;
    String key = client + ":" + minute;
    int count = counters.merge(key, 1, Integer::sum);
    return count <= limit;
}
```

The sliding window algorithm fixes the boundary flaw by considering a rolling window. A practical variant, sliding window counter, blends the current and previous window counts weighted by how far into the current window you are, giving a smooth approximation without storing every timestamp.

The token bucket algorithm models a bucket that refills at a steady rate up to a capacity. Each request removes one token; if the bucket is empty, the request is rejected. This elegantly allows short bursts up to the bucket capacity while enforcing a long-term average equal to the refill rate, which is why it is the most popular choice for APIs.

```java
// Token bucket: refill by elapsed time, consume one token per request
public synchronized boolean allowToken(String client, double ratePerSec, double capacity) {
    Bucket b = buckets.computeIfAbsent(client, k -> new Bucket(capacity, System.nanoTime()));
    long now = System.nanoTime();
    double elapsedSec = (now - b.lastRefill) / 1e9;
    b.tokens = Math.min(capacity, b.tokens + elapsedSec * ratePerSec); // refill
    b.lastRefill = now;
    if (b.tokens >= 1) { b.tokens -= 1; return true; }                 // consume
    return false;                                                       // throttle
}
```

For distributed rate limiting, the counter or bucket state cannot live in one server's memory, because a client could spread requests across servers and bypass the limit. So the state lives in Redis, and the check-and-decrement is done atomically, usually with a small Lua script so the read, compute, and write happen as one operation and two servers cannot both see the last token. A senior nuance is where to place the limiter: at the API gateway so bad traffic is rejected before it reaches your services, returning HTTP 429 Too Many Requests with a Retry-After header, and using different limits per endpoint since a cheap balance read can tolerate a far higher rate than an expensive transfer.

