sections.s2_technical:
The foundation is asynchronous decoupling. A producer like the transfer service publishes a notification event to a message queue such as Kafka or SQS and returns immediately. Consumers read from the queue and do the slow work of calling external providers. This means a slow SMS gateway never slows a money transfer, and a burst of notifications is absorbed by the queue rather than overwhelming the providers.

```java
// Producer: transfer service just publishes an event, returns fast
notificationQueue.publish(new NotificationEvent(
    "SAV2001", NotificationType.TRANSFER_SUCCESS,
    Map.of("amount", "50000", "to", "CUR3001"), Priority.HIGH));
```

Channel selection comes from user preferences. Each user record stores which channels they opted into and in what order; some want push first then SMS fallback, others want email only. The system respects do-not-disturb windows and regulatory opt-outs. Content is produced by a template engine so that the message "Dear {name}, Rs.{amount} transferred to {to}" is filled with real values at send time, keeping messages consistent and translatable.

```java
// Template rendering fills placeholders from event data
String body = templateEngine.render("TRANSFER_SUCCESS",
    Map.of("name", "Rajesh", "amount", "50000", "to", "CUR3001"));
// → "Dear Rajesh, Rs.50000 transferred to CUR3001"
```

Priority handling means critical messages like a fraud alert jump ahead of marketing messages. This is done with separate queues or priority lanes so a promotional blast never delays a security alert. Retry logic handles transient provider failures with exponential backoff and a maximum attempt count; messages that still fail go to a dead-letter queue for investigation rather than being lost. A senior nuance is idempotency and deduplication: each notification carries a stable id so that if a consumer crashes after sending but before acknowledging, the redelivery is recognized and the customer is not texted twice. Delivery status is tracked at each stage so operations can see how many messages are queued, sent, delivered, or failed.

