// ─── File: CacheService.java ───
package com.bank.cache;

import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.Lock;
import java.util.logging.*;

public class CacheService {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger("Cache");
    private final Database db;
    private final ConsistentHashRing ring = new ConsistentHashRing();
    private final Map<String, LruCache> nodes = new HashMap<>();
    private final StampedeGuard guard = new StampedeGuard();

    public CacheService(Database db, int perNodeCapacity) {
        this.db = db;
        for (String n : List.of("cache-A", "cache-B", "cache-C")) {
            ring.addNode(n);
            nodes.put(n, new LruCache(perNodeCapacity));
        }
    }
    public String nodeFor(String key) { return ring.nodeFor(key); }
    private LruCache cacheOf(String key) { return nodes.get(nodeFor(key)); }

    // cache-aside read
    public String read(String key) {
        LruCache c = cacheOf(key);
        String v = c.get(key);
        if (v != null) { log.fine(() -> "HIT " + key); return v; }
        v = db.load(key);
        if (v != null) c.put(key, v);
        return v;
    }

    // write-through: DB first (durable), then cache (fresh)
    public void writeThrough(String key, String value) {
        db.save(key, value);
        cacheOf(key).put(key, value);
        log.info(() -> "write-through " + key + "=" + value);
    }

    public void invalidate(String key) {
        cacheOf(key).remove(key);
        log.info(() -> "invalidated " + key);
    }

    // read with single-flight stampede protection
    public String readWithGuard(String key) {
        LruCache c = cacheOf(key);
        String v = c.get(key);
        if (v != null) return v;              // fast path
        Lock lock = guard.lockFor(key);
        lock.lock();
        try {
            v = c.get(key);                    // double-check after acquiring
            if (v != null) { log.info(() -> "served by waiter " + key); return v; }
            log.info(() -> "single rebuild of " + key);
            v = db.load(key);                  // only ONE thread hits DB
            c.put(key, v);
            return v;
        } finally { lock.unlock(); }
    }
}
```

sections.s2_technical:
Chunked upload splits a large file into fixed-size pieces, say four megabytes each, uploaded independently. This makes uploads resumable: if the network drops after chunk seven of ten, the client resends only chunks eight through ten rather than the whole file. Each chunk is identified by the hash of its content.

```java
// Split a file into content-hashed chunks for resumable upload
public List<Chunk> chunk(byte[] file, int chunkSize) {
    List<Chunk> chunks = new ArrayList<>();
    for (int off = 0; off < file.length; off += chunkSize) {
        byte[] data = Arrays.copyOfRange(file, off, Math.min(off + chunkSize, file.length));
        chunks.add(new Chunk(sha256(data), data));  // id = content hash
    }
    return chunks;
}
```

Deduplication uses those content hashes. Before storing a chunk, the system checks whether a chunk with that hash already exists; if so, it simply references the existing one instead of storing a duplicate. Because two identical documents or two copies of the same attachment produce identical chunk hashes, this saves enormous space across millions of files.

The metadata database is kept separate from the blob store. The blob store holds raw chunk bytes keyed by hash, while the metadata database records which chunks, in which order, form each file, plus owner, size, timestamps, version, and permissions. This separation lets the blob store scale for throughput while the metadata layer stays relationally queryable.

```java
// Metadata record ties a logical file to its ordered chunk hashes
public record FileMetadata(String fileId, String owner, long version,
                           List<String> chunkHashes, Set<String> readers) {}
```

Access control checks on every read and write whether the requesting user is the owner or in the allowed readers, returning forbidden otherwise. Versioning stores a new metadata record for each change while old chunks remain referenced, so a previous version can be restored. A senior nuance is download delivery: popular files are served through a content delivery network with signed, time-limited URLs so the origin is not overloaded and only authorized users can fetch, and uploads go directly to the blob store via pre-signed URLs so file bytes never pass through the application servers.

