sections.s2_technical:
The trie is a tree where each edge is a character and each path from the root spells a prefix. To find completions for a prefix, you walk the trie following each character, and the node you land on represents that prefix. Below that node lie all queries starting with the prefix.

```java
// Walk the trie to the node representing a prefix
private TrieNode walk(String prefix) {
    TrieNode node = root;
    for (char c : prefix.toCharArray()) {
        node = node.children.get(c);
        if (node == null) return null;   // no such prefix
    }
    return node;
}
```

Returning the top-K matches by naively scanning every descendant would be too slow for a huge vocabulary. The optimization is to store, at each node, a precomputed list of the top-K most popular completions beneath it. Then answering a prefix query is just walking to the node and returning its cached list, which is near-constant time regardless of how many queries share the prefix.

```java
// Each node caches its top-K completions by popularity
class TrieNode {
    Map<Character, TrieNode> children = new HashMap<>();
    List<String> topK = new ArrayList<>();  // precomputed hot suggestions
}
```

Popularity comes from query logs: each time a query is searched, its frequency increases, and that bubbles up to the top-K lists of all its prefixes. Because recomputing the whole trie on every search is expensive, updates are batched: a background job periodically rebuilds or adjusts the top-K lists from recent logs, so the suggestions stay fresh without slowing reads.

Caching adds another layer: the most frequently typed prefixes, like single letters, are cached in a fast store so even the trie walk is skipped. A senior nuance is the trade-off between freshness and read speed. Reads must be instant, so the system accepts that a brand-new trending query may take a few minutes to appear in suggestions, which is eventual consistency applied to rankings. For a bank, suggestions are also filtered by context and permissions, so a customer never sees a suggestion for an admin-only action.

