sections.s2_technical:
Job storage is durable because the schedule must survive restarts. Each job record holds its id, the time it is due, a payload describing the work, its tenant, its state, and retry bookkeeping. A time-based index, such as ordering by next-run time, lets the scheduler efficiently ask "which jobs are due now."

```java
// Fetch jobs whose due time has passed, oldest first
public List<Job> dueJobs(long now, int limit) {
    return store.values().stream()
        .filter(j -> j.state() == State.PENDING && j.dueAt() <= now)
        .sorted(Comparator.comparingLong(Job::dueAt))
        .limit(limit).toList();
}
```

Execution guarantees are the heart of the design. At-least-once means a job will run even if a worker crashes mid-execution, achieved by a worker claiming a job with a lease and the job being re-dispatched if the lease expires without a completion acknowledgement. Because at-least-once can run a job twice (a worker completes but crashes before acknowledging), the job must be idempotent or deduplicated: a stable execution id lets the system recognize a repeat and skip the duplicate effect.

```java
// Claim a job atomically with a lease so two workers can't both run it
public boolean claim(String jobId, String workerId, long leaseMs) {
    Job j = store.get(jobId);
    if (j.state() != State.PENDING) return false;        // someone else claimed
    j.setState(State.RUNNING);
    j.setLeaseOwner(workerId);
    j.setLeaseExpiry(System.currentTimeMillis() + leaseMs);
    return true;
}
```

Failure retry re-schedules a failed job after a backoff delay, up to a maximum attempt count, after which it goes to a dead-letter state for investigation rather than retrying forever. Multi-tenant isolation ensures fairness: jobs carry a tenant id, and the dispatcher caps how many jobs a single tenant can occupy at once, or uses per-tenant queues, so a tenant that floods the system with jobs does not starve others. A senior nuance is handling clock skew and the lease carefully: leases must be long enough to cover legitimate execution but short enough to recover quickly from a dead worker, and completion must be recorded atomically with releasing the lease so a job is never both marked done and re-dispatched.

