sections.s2_technical:
The core job is distribution. Without a load balancer, you can only scale up a single server, which has a hard ceiling and a single point of failure. A load balancer lets you scale out across many servers and survive the loss of any one.

Layer 4 versus Layer 7 is about how much the balancer inspects. A Layer 4 balancer works at the transport layer and forwards packets based on IP address and port without reading the request body, which is extremely fast and protocol-agnostic. A Layer 7 balancer works at the application layer and can read the HTTP request, so it can route based on the URL path, cookies, or headers, enabling features like sending transfer requests to one service and statement requests to another.

```java
// L7 routing by path: pick a backend pool based on the request URL
public String routeL7(String path) {
    if (path.startsWith("/api/transfers")) return "transfer-pool";
    if (path.startsWith("/api/statements")) return "statement-pool";
    return "default-pool";
}
```

The distribution algorithm decides which backend gets the next request. Round-robin cycles through servers in order, which is simple and fair when servers are equal. Least-connections sends to the server with the fewest active connections, which is better when requests vary in duration. IP hash maps a client's IP to a consistent backend, giving session stickiness so a client keeps hitting the same server.

```java
// Round-robin and least-connections selection
public String roundRobin(List<String> servers) {
    return servers.get(counter.getAndIncrement() % servers.size());
}
public String leastConnections(Map<String,Integer> activeConns) {
    return activeConns.entrySet().stream()
        .min(Map.Entry.comparingByValue()).map(Map.Entry::getKey).orElseThrow();
}
```

Health checks keep the pool honest. The balancer periodically probes each backend, often with an HTTP check on a health endpoint, and removes any server that fails from rotation until it recovers, so clients never hit a dead server. A senior nuance is session affinity versus statelessness: sticky sessions via IP hash simplify stateful apps but hurt even distribution and complicate failover, so the better pattern is to keep servers stateless and store session data in a shared cache, letting the balancer freely distribute. Load balancers also terminate TLS, absorb bursts, and are themselves made highly available in pairs so the balancer is not a new single point of failure.

