sections.s2_technical:
CloudFront sits in front of one or more origins. An origin is where the real content lives: an S3 bucket for static files, an Application Load Balancer or EC2 for dynamic APIs. A CloudFront distribution is the configuration that ties edge locations to those origins.

Cache behaviors are the rules engine. Each behavior matches a path pattern, such as images under a static path or calls under an API path, and maps it to an origin with its own settings. You set the time-to-live controlling how long an object stays cached, the cache key deciding which request attributes make objects distinct such as query strings or specific headers, and origin groups for failover so that if the primary origin fails, CloudFront automatically serves from a secondary origin.

```java
// Conceptual mapping of CloudFront cache behaviors (as config, shown in Java-like pseudo)
behavior("/static/*", origin("s3-assets"), ttlSeconds(86400), cacheKey("url"));
behavior("/api/*",    origin("alb-api"),    ttlSeconds(0),     cacheKey("url+auth-header"));
originGroup("api-with-failover", primary("alb-api"), secondary("alb-api-dr"));
```

Invalidation removes cached objects before their time-to-live expires, issued as an invalidation request listing paths; however, versioned object names are preferred for frequent changes because invalidations have cost and propagation delay. Access restriction protects private content. Signed URLs and signed cookies grant time-limited access so only authorized users fetch premium reports, and Origin Access Control locks an S3 bucket so it is reachable only through CloudFront and not directly, preventing people from bypassing the CDN and its security.

```java
// Signed URL concept: a time-limited, tamper-proof link to premium content
String signedUrl = cloudFront.signedUrl(
    "/reports/premium/portfolio-SAV2001.pdf",
    Instant.now().plus(Duration.ofMinutes(10)));  // expires in 10 minutes
```

Lambda@Edge and CloudFront Functions run code at the edge to transform requests and responses, for example adding security headers, rewriting URLs, or doing lightweight authentication close to the user. API acceleration works because even uncacheable dynamic API traffic benefits from CloudFront's optimized, persistent connections from the edge to the origin over Amazon's backbone, reducing round-trip latency. A senior nuance is that CloudFront integrates with AWS Shield for automatic DDoS protection and with AWS WAF for request filtering, so the distributed edge absorbs and filters attacks far from the origin, which is a major security win for a bank.

