sections.s2_technical:
Cache-aside, also called lazy loading, is the most common read strategy. On a read, the application checks the cache; on a miss it loads from the database and populates the cache, so only requested data is cached. Its weakness is that the first read is slow and, without invalidation, the cache can go stale after a database write.

```java
// Cache-aside read
public String read(String key) {
    String v = cache.get(key);
    if (v == null) { v = db.load(key); cache.put(key, v); } // lazy load on miss
    return v;
}
```

Write-through writes to the cache and the database together on every write, so the cache is always consistent with the database. Writes are slightly slower because two systems are updated, but reads never see stale data. This is my default for balances in banking, where correctness beats a little write latency.

```java
// Write-through: DB first (durable), then cache (consistent)
public void write(String key, String value) {
    db.save(key, value);
    cache.put(key, value);
}
```

Write-behind writes to the cache immediately and queues the database write to happen asynchronously. Writes are very fast and can be batched, but if the cache node crashes before flushing, recent writes are lost, which makes write-behind unsuitable for money and acceptable only for tolerant data like analytics counters.

Invalidation keeps the cache correct. A time-to-live lets entries expire automatically, so stale data self-heals after a bounded delay, which is simple but allows brief staleness. Event-based invalidation deletes or updates the cache entry the moment the underlying data changes, which is precise but requires wiring the write path to the cache. Stampede, or thundering herd, happens when a very popular key expires and many requests miss simultaneously and all hit the database. I prevent it with a single-flight lock so only one request rebuilds the value while others wait, plus staggered or probabilistic early expiration. A senior nuance is that these strategies combine: cache-aside reads with write-through or event-based invalidation and stampede protection on the hottest keys gives both speed and correctness.

