// ─── File: ShardRouter.java ───
package com.bank.sharding;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.*;

public class ShardRouter {
    private final int virtualShards;
    private final List<String> physical = new ArrayList<>();
    // virtual shard index -> physical shard name
    private final Map<Integer, String> vToPhysical = new HashMap<>();
    private final Map<String, Shard> shards = new HashMap<>();

    public ShardRouter(int virtualShards) { this.virtualShards = virtualShards; }

    public void addPhysicalShard(String name) {
        physical.add(name);
        shards.put(name, new Shard(name));
        rebalanceVirtualShards();   // reassign vshards evenly; moves only some
    }

    // spread virtual shards across physical shards round-robin
    private void rebalanceVirtualShards() {
        for (int v = 0; v < virtualShards; v++)
            vToPhysical.put(v, physical.get(v % physical.size()));
    }

    private int virtualFor(String accountId) {
        return Math.abs(accountId.hashCode()) % virtualShards;  // key → virtual shard
    }
    public String physicalFor(String accountId) {
        return vToPhysical.get(virtualFor(accountId));
    }
    public Account account(String accountId) {
        return shards.get(physicalFor(accountId)).account(accountId);
    }

    // cross-shard scatter-gather
    public BigDecimal totalBalanceAcrossShards() {
        return shards.values().stream().map(Shard::sumBalances)
            .reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add);
    }

    public Map<String,String> virtualAssignments() { return new HashMap<>(vToPhysical); }
    public int countReassigned(Map<String,String> before) {
        int moved = 0;
        for (int v = 0; v < virtualShards; v++)
            if (!before.get(String.valueOf(v)).equals(vToPhysical.get(v))) moved++;
        return moved;
    }
}
```

sections.s2_technical:
Master-slave, also called primary-replica, has one node that accepts all writes and streams its change log to read-only replicas. Reads are spread across replicas to scale read-heavy workloads, while writes stay on the single primary, which keeps writes consistent and simple. This is the most common pattern and fits banking well because most traffic is reads like balance checks and statements.

```java
// Route writes to primary, reads to a replica (read scaling)
public void write(String sql) { primary.execute(sql); }
public String read(String sql) { return pickReplica().query(sql); }
```

Replication lag is the delay before a write on the primary appears on a replica. Asynchronous replication acknowledges the write as soon as the primary commits, giving low write latency but allowing a replica to be briefly behind, which can cause a read-your-own-write anomaly where a user does not immediately see their own change on a replica. Semi-synchronous replication waits for at least one replica to acknowledge, trading a little latency for stronger guarantees.

```java
// Read-your-writes: route a user's read to the primary right after their write
public String readAfterWrite(String sql, boolean justWrote) {
    return justWrote ? primary.query(sql) : pickReplica().query(sql);
}
```

Master-master lets multiple nodes accept writes, improving write availability and allowing writes near each region, but it introduces conflicts when two nodes modify the same row concurrently. Conflicts must be resolved by a strategy such as last-write-wins using timestamps, which can lose data, or application-specific merging. For money, master-master is dangerous because conflicting balance writes are unacceptable, so banks usually keep a single writer per piece of data.

Failover promotes a replica to primary when the primary fails. It must detect the failure, choose the most up-to-date replica, redirect writes to it, and fence off the old primary so it cannot resume and cause split-brain, where two nodes both believe they are primary. A senior nuance is that automatic failover must guard against split-brain with quorums or fencing, and that the choice between asynchronous and synchronous replication is a direct trade-off between write latency and the risk of losing recently acknowledged writes on failover, a trade-off a bank resolves toward durability.

