```java
// ─── File: ReplicationManager.java ───
package com.bank.replication;

import java.util.*;
import java.util.logging.*;

public class ReplicationManager {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger("Repl");
    private Node primary;
    private final List<Node> replicas;
    private final Deque<String[]> replicationQueue = new ArrayDeque<>(); // pending changes
    private int rrIndex = 0;

    public ReplicationManager(Node primary, List<Node> replicas) {
        this.primary = primary; this.replicas = new ArrayList<>(replicas);
    }

    // writes go ONLY to the primary; queued for async replication
    public void write(String key, String value) {
        if (primary.isFenced()) throw new IllegalStateException("primary is fenced");
        primary.apply(key, value);
        replicationQueue.add(new String[]{key, value});
        log.info(() -> "WRITE to " + primary.name() + " " + key + "=" + value);
    }

    // apply queued changes to replicas (simulates catching up lag)
    public void replicate() {
        while (!replicationQueue.isEmpty()) {
            String[] c = replicationQueue.poll();
            for (Node r : replicas) r.apply(c[0], c[1]);
        }
        log.info("replicas caught up");
    }

    // read-your-writes: route to primary if the caller just wrote
    public String read(String key, boolean justWrote) {
        if (justWrote) return primary.read(key);
        Node r = replicas.get(rrIndex++ % replicas.size());  // round-robin read scaling
        return r.read(key);
    }

    public void failover() {
        Node newPrimary = new Failover().promote(primary, replicas);
        replicas.remove(newPrimary);
        replicas.add(primary);     // old primary, once recovered, rejoins as replica
        this.primary = newPrimary;
    }
}
```

sections.s2_technical:
The three properties are consistency, meaning every read sees the most recent write; availability, meaning every request gets a non-error response; and partition tolerance, meaning the system keeps working despite dropped messages between nodes. The theorem's real meaning is often misunderstood: partition tolerance is not optional in a distributed system because networks do fail, so the practical choice is only between consistency and availability during a partition.

A CP system, when it cannot guarantee that a node has the latest data because it is cut off, refuses to serve rather than serve possibly wrong data. This preserves correctness at the cost of some unavailability during the partition.

```java
// CP: refuse to serve if this node might be behind during a partition
public String readCP(String key) {
    if (partitioned && !isLeaderReachable())
        throw new ServiceUnavailableException("rejecting read to preserve consistency");
    return store.get(key);  // only serve when we can guarantee freshness
}
```

An AP system keeps answering even when partitioned, returning whatever data the reachable node has, which may be stale, and reconciling the divergent copies once the partition heals through eventual consistency.

```java
// AP: always answer, even if possibly stale; reconcile later
public String readAP(String key) {
    return store.getOrDefault(key, lastKnown.get(key));  // never errors, may be stale
}
```

The domain decides the choice. A social media feed, a product catalogue, or a trending leaderboard can tolerate briefly stale data, so they choose AP for maximum availability and reconcile in the background, which is why most large consumer systems are AP with eventual consistency. A bank ledger, an inventory count that must not oversell, or a uniqueness constraint cannot tolerate serving wrong data, so they choose CP and accept that some operations are rejected during a partition. A senior nuance is that the choice is per-operation, not per-system: the same bank can serve a cached, possibly slightly stale balance display with AP semantics for responsiveness, while insisting on CP for the actual debit and credit that move money, which is a tunable consistency approach rather than a single global decision.

